<article xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" dtd-version="1.3" xml:lang="en" article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><processing-meta base-tagset="archiving" mathml-version="3.0" table-model="xhtml" tagset-family="jats"><restricted-by>pmc</restricted-by></processing-meta><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">J Psychiatry Neurosci</journal-id><journal-id journal-id-type="iso-abbrev">J Psychiatry Neurosci</journal-id><journal-id journal-id-type="hwp">jpn</journal-id><journal-title-group><journal-title>Journal of Psychiatry &#x00026; Neuroscience : JPN</journal-title></journal-title-group><issn pub-type="ppub">1180-4882</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1488-2434</issn><publisher><publisher-name>CMA Impact Inc.</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta>
<article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">40268326</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">12029312</article-id>
<article-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1503/jpn.240112</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="publisher-id">50-2-E126</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Research Paper</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>Joint and distinct neural structure and function deficits in major depressive disorder with suicidality: a multimodal meta-analysis of MRI studies</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author" equal-contrib="yes"><name><surname>Lin</surname><given-names>Shiqi</given-names></name><degrees>MMed</degrees><xref rid="fn1-50-2-e126" ref-type="author-notes">*</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author" equal-contrib="yes"><name><surname>Chen</surname><given-names>Ziqi</given-names></name><degrees>MD</degrees><xref rid="fn1-50-2-e126" ref-type="author-notes">*</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Zhao</surname><given-names>Youjin</given-names></name><degrees>MD</degrees></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author" corresp="yes"><name><surname>Gong</surname><given-names>Qiyong</given-names></name><degrees>MD, PhD</degrees></contrib><aff id="af1-50-2-e126">From the Xiamen Key Laboratory of Psychoradiology and Neuromodulation, Department of Radiology, West China Xiamen Hospital of Sichuan University, Xiamen, Fujian, China (Lin, Gong); the Department of Radiology, Huaxi MR Research Center (HMRRC), West China Hospital of Sichuan University, Chengdu, Sichuan, China (Chen, Zhao, Gong); the Research Unit of Psychoradiology, Chinese Academy of Medical Sciences, Chengdu, Sichuan, China (Chen); the Functional and Molecular Imaging Key Laboratory of Sichuan Province, West China Hospital of Sichuan University, Chengdu, Sichuan, China (Zhao)</aff></contrib-group><author-notes><corresp id="c1-50-2-e126">Correspondence to: Q. Gong, Department of Radiology, West China Xiamen Hospital of Sichuan University, 699 Jinyuan Xi Road, Jimei District, 361021 Xiamen, Fujian, China; <email>qiyonggong@hmrrc.org.cn</email></corresp><fn id="fn1-50-2-e126"><label>*</label><p>These authors contributed equally to this work.</p></fn></author-notes><pub-date pub-type="collection"><season>Mar-Apr</season><year>2025</year></pub-date><pub-date pub-type="epub"><day>23</day><month>4</month><year>2025</year></pub-date><volume>50</volume><issue>2</issue><fpage>E126</fpage><lpage>E141</lpage><history><date date-type="received"><day>10</day><month>9</month><year>2024</year></date><date date-type="rev-recd"><day>02</day><month>1</month><year>2025</year></date><date date-type="accepted"><day>04</day><month>2</month><year>2025</year></date></history><permissions><copyright-statement>&#x000a9; 2025 CMA Impact Inc. or its licensors</copyright-statement><copyright-year>2025</copyright-year><license><ali:license_ref xmlns:ali="http://www.niso.org/schemas/ali/1.0/" specific-use="textmining" content-type="ccbyncndlicense">https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/</ali:license_ref><license-p>This is an Open Access article distributed in accordance with the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution (CC BY-NC-ND 4.0) licence, which permits use, distribution and reproduction in any medium, provided that the original publication is properly cited, the use is noncommercial (i.e., research or educational use), and no modifications or adaptations are made. See: <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/">https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/</ext-link></license-p></license></permissions><abstract><sec><title>Background:</title><p>Suicide risk is a major concern for patients with major depressive disorder (MDD). Neuroimaging studies have demonstrated that patients with MDD with suicidal ideation or suicide attempt (MDD-S) are accompanied by neurostructural or functional abnormalities, but there is no consensus of opinion on neural substrate alterations involved in MDD-S.</p></sec><sec><title>Methods:</title><p>We performed a whole-brain multimodal meta-analysis of existing magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) studies to identify conjoint and separate alterations of grey matter volume (GMV) and spontaneous brain activity characteristics (regional homogeneity and amplitude of low-frequency fluctuations) between patients with MDD-S and patients with MDD without suicidal ideation or suicidal attempt (MDD-NS) via the seed-based <italic>d</italic> mapping software. We excluded studies that used other modalities, had overlapping data, or had insufficient information.</p></sec><sec><title>Results:</title><p>Our systematic search identified 13 structural MRI studies (471 patients with MDD-S and 508 patients with MDD-NS) and 16 resting-state functional MRI studies (704 patients with MDD-S and 554 patients with MDD-NS) published up to Dec. 5, 2023. Compared with patients with MDD-NS, those with MDD-S showed increased GMV with hypoactivity in the left postcentral gyrus, decreased GMV with hypoactivity in the right inferior parietal gyri, decreased GMV with hyperactivity in the right insula, and separate GMV and functional changes within the bilateral parietal, occipital, and frontal lobes, and the left thalamus.</p></sec><sec><title>Limitations:</title><p>We were unable to analyze the association between brain features and clinical detail because of a lack of data. Included studies showed considerable heterogeneity and publication bias.</p></sec><sec><title>Conclusion:</title><p>These findings provide a comprehensive overview of brain morphological and spontaneous functional impairments linked to impulsivity, impaired positive reward modulation, emotional disturbances, abnormal emotional processing, and cognitive deficits in MDD-S. These results support an understanding of the relationship between neural substrates and clinical symptoms in MDD-S, and these alterations provide useful insight into pathophysiological mechanisms and intervention strategies to decrease suicide risk in MDD.</p></sec></abstract><funding-group><award-group><funding-source id="CS1">National Key R&#x00026;D Program of China</funding-source><award-id award-type="contract" rid="CS1">2022YFC2009900</award-id></award-group><award-group><funding-source id="CS2">National Natural Science Foundation of China</funding-source><award-id award-type="contract" rid="CS2">81820108018</award-id><award-id award-type="contract" rid="CS2">81761128023</award-id><award-id award-type="contract" rid="CS2">82027808</award-id></award-group></funding-group></article-meta></front><body><sec sec-type="intro"><title>Introduction</title><p>Major depressive disorder (MDD) is a common chronic psychiatric disorder with a high disease burden.<xref rid="b1-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">1</xref>,<xref rid="b2-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">2</xref> It involves a severe disturbance in mood, interests, concentration, and cognition,<xref rid="b3-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">3</xref> and affects nearly 350 million people worldwide.<xref rid="b4-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">4</xref> The major concern or disease burden of MDD is attributed to its increased tendency of suicidal ideation and suicidal behaviour, including suicide death and suicide attempt.<xref rid="b3-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">3</xref>,<xref rid="b5-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">5</xref> It was reported that 7% of males and 4% of females with MDD died from suicide every year,<xref rid="b6-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">6</xref> and suicide risk of patients with MDD was estimated to be 20-fold higher than that of the general population.<xref rid="b7-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">7</xref> Therefore, for suicide prevention, it is urgent to identify patients with MDD with vulnerability to suicide and to explore underlying pathological mechanisms. The development of suicide risk is complex, involving contributions from clinical, psychological, biological, social, and environmental factors. Clinically, it is a difficult task to assess and predict suicidality in patients with MDD, given the complexity of the development of suicidality, which is related to comorbid psychiatric illness, diagnostic subtype, clinical symptoms, clinical course, clinical assessment scales, and patient willingness to collaborate with clinicians.<xref rid="b8-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">8</xref>&#x02013;<xref rid="b13-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">13</xref> In terms of biological mechanisms, some evidence has suggested that suicidal etiopathogenesis is associated with neuroinflammation<xref rid="b14-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">14</xref> and neurotransmitters,<xref rid="b15-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">15</xref>,<xref rid="b16-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">16</xref> while the precise molecular mechanisms underlying MDD with suicidality are poorly understood. By this token, determining objective biomarkers for identification of suicidality among patients with MDD and the neurophysiology of suicidal vulnerability will be beneficial and shed light on potential therapeutic targets.</p><p>With advances in neuroimaging techniques, biomarkers from neuroimaging studies are vital in providing new information for pathogenesis and achieving precision medicine for many neuropsychiatric disorders.<xref rid="b17-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">17</xref>,<xref rid="b18-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">18</xref> In the last 2 decades, structural magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) has been used to investigate the structural macroscopic variations in grey matter, such as volume, thickness, and surface.<xref rid="b19-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">19</xref>,<xref rid="b20-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">20</xref> Voxel-based morphometry is a classical whole-brain analytic approach for quantifying abnormalities in grey matter volume (GMV) in an automated, comprehensive, objective, and repeatable way.<xref rid="b21-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">21</xref>,<xref rid="b22-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">22</xref> Surface-based morphometry is also an effective method for estimating the indices of cortical morphology, such as cortical thickness, cortical surface area, and gyrification.<xref rid="b23-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">23</xref>,<xref rid="b24-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">24</xref> MRI approaches for identifying brain functional alterations include resting-state functional MRI (rs-fMRI) and task-based functional MRI (task-fMRI).<xref rid="b25-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">25</xref> Task-fMRI identifies some experimental behavioural condition&#x02013;evoked alteration patterns of brain activation,<xref rid="b26-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">26</xref> while rs-fMRI has been widely applied to detect spontaneous neural activity and further integrate neural networks via blood oxygen level&#x02013;dependent (BOLD) signals.<xref rid="b27-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">27</xref>,<xref rid="b28-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">28</xref> Compared with task-fMRI, rs-fMRI studies reflect more traits, more experiment repeatability, and fewer potential confounding factors related to cognitive activation.<xref rid="b29-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">29</xref>&#x02013;<xref rid="b31-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">31</xref> Analytic methods to depict the local features of the BOLD signals include regional homogeneity and amplitude of low-frequency fluctuation (ALFF). In terms of structural changes, previous studies have reported GMV abnormalities in the left angular gyrus,<xref rid="b32-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">32</xref>,<xref rid="b33-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">33</xref> right middle temporal gyrus,<xref rid="b33-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">33</xref>,<xref rid="b34-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">34</xref> and right inferior frontal gyrus (IFG)<xref rid="b35-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">35</xref>,<xref rid="b36-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">36</xref> among patients with MDD with suicide attempt or suicidal ideation compared with those without suicide attempt or suicidal ideation. However, the results of some studies are different. One study found decreased GMV of the right orbitofrontal area among patients with MDD with suicide attempt,<xref rid="b35-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">35</xref> whereas another study observed increased GMV of the left orbitofrontal area.<xref rid="b37-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">37</xref> In addition, decreased GMV in different hemispheres of the cerebellum were detected in distinct studies.<xref rid="b32-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">32</xref>,<xref rid="b38-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">38</xref></p><p>In terms of functional analysis, several studies have observed spontaneous brain activity abnormalities in several brain regions among patients with MDD with suicide attempt or suicidal ideation compared with those without, such as in the right hippocampus,<xref rid="b39-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">39</xref>,<xref rid="b40-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">40</xref> right insula,<xref rid="b41-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">41</xref>,<xref rid="b42-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">42</xref> right precentral gyrus (preCG),<xref rid="b40-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">40</xref>,<xref rid="b42-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">42</xref> and right fusiform gyrus,<xref rid="b40-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">40</xref>,<xref rid="b43-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">43</xref> while other studies have had contradictory findings. For example, some studies found increased fractional ALFF and regional homogeneity in the right insula among patients with MDD with suicide attempt,<xref rid="b42-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">42</xref> whereas another study observed decreased dynamic ALFF in the same region.<xref rid="b41-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">41</xref> In addition, increased fractional ALFF and regional homogeneity,<xref rid="b42-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">42</xref> and decreased ALFF in the right preCG were also detected in different studies. <xref rid="b40-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">40</xref> The reasons for variable and inconsistent findings may be related to the clinical and demographic characteristics of participants, small sample sizes, and data analytical protocols. <xref rid="b22-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">22</xref>,<xref rid="b44-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">44</xref> The close integration of structural and functional abnormalities has been extensively documented in neuropsychiatric diseases, such as Alzheimer disease,<xref rid="b45-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">45</xref> multiple sclerosis,<xref rid="b46-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">46</xref>,<xref rid="b47-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">47</xref> schizophrenia,<xref rid="b48-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">48</xref> and MDD.<xref rid="b49-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">49</xref> However, it is currently unclear whether brain regions with both functional and structural abnormalities occur among patients with MDD with suicidal attempt or ideation.</p><p>Neuroimaging meta-analysis can impartially integrate findings from extensive studies. Other meta-analyses have reported structural and functional brain abnormalities in suicidal ideation or behaviour. A meta-analysis of functional alterations in emotional and cognitive tasks (7 task-fMRI studies including 150 patients with MDD with suicide attempt and 216 patients with MDD without suicide attempt) showed different activation in the left insula and bilateral fusiform gyrus among those with suicide attempt compared with those without, which indicated that abnormal emotion regulation, processing of negative information, and self-awareness caused by these altered brain regions was related to suicidal behaviour in MDD.<xref rid="b50-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">50</xref> A multimodal meta-analysis identified brain regions with functional and structural abnormalities (12 studies encompassing 213 participants with a history of a psychiatric disorder and suicide attempt and 262 participants with a history of a psychiatric disorder without suicide attempt), including the superior temporal gyrus (STG), rectal gyrus, anterior and posterior cingulate cortices, and caudate nucleus (CAU) in those with suicide attempt, which suggested that increased suicidal behaviour was related to reduced motivational control over the intentional behavioural reaction to negative stimuli.<xref rid="b51-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">51</xref> A meta-analysis of rs-fMRI and task-fMRI studies (17 studies including 381 participants with suicide attempt or suicidal ideation and different mental disorders and 642 controls) found dysfunctional brain regions among participants with suicide attempt and suicidal ideation, including the STG, middle occipital gyrus (MOG), and left insula, which were related to disturbance of impulsivity, emotion and cognitive processing, and mental pain in suicide.<xref rid="b52-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">52</xref> However, the mechanism of suicide attempt may vary among different mental illnesses.<xref rid="b53-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">53</xref> It remains unanswered whether GMV and resting-state functional changes are specific to patients with MDD with suicide attempt or suicidal ideation and whether such specific GMV changes converge with function changes.</p><p>In this study we sought to categorize patients with MDD with suicide attempt or suicidal ideation as patients with suicidality (MDD-S) and those without suicide attempt or suicidal ideation as patients without suicidality (MDD-NS). We performed a meta-analysis to synthesize findings of studies involving patients with MDD-S, identifying specific functional and structural abnormalities and colocalization of structural and functional abnormalities. Findings could contribute to comprehensive analysis of the brain changes related to MDD-S, detection of objective biomarkers for MDDS, and understanding of the pathophysiology of MDD-S.</p></sec><sec sec-type="methods"><title>Methods</title><sec><title>Literature search strategy and selection criteria</title><p>We conducted the literature search using the PubMed, Web of Science, Embase, Chinese National Knowledge Infrastructure, and WanFang databases. We searched for studies published before Dec. 5, 2023. Candidate structural MRI studies were sought using the keywords &#x0201c;suicide&#x0201d; or &#x0201c;suicidal,&#x0201d; &#x0201c;imaging&#x0201d; or &#x0201c;magnetic resonance imaging&#x0201d; or &#x0201c;MRI,&#x0201d; &#x0201c;voxel-based morphometry&#x0201d; or &#x0201c;VBM&#x0201d; or &#x0201c;grey matter volume,&#x0201d; and &#x0201c;depression&#x0201d; or &#x0201c;depressive disorder.&#x0201d; Candidate fMRI studies were sought using the keywords &#x0201c;suicide&#x0201d; or &#x0201c;suicidal,&#x0201d; &#x0201c;imaging&#x0201d; or &#x0201c;magnetic resonance imaging&#x0201d; or &#x0201c;MRI,&#x0201d; &#x0201c;functional MRI&#x0201d; or &#x0201c;functional magnetic resonance imaging&#x0201d; or &#x0201c;fMRI,&#x0201d; and &#x0201c;depression&#x0201d; or &#x0201c;depressive disorder.&#x0201d;</p><p>The criteria for eligible studies were that they were published in English or Chinese journals with peer review, they compared alterations in GMV or resting-state brain activity between MDD-S and MDD-NS groups at the whole brain level, and they reported results with Montreal Neurological Institute (MNI) or Talairach coordinates. We excluded studies that reported only region-of-interest findings or functional connectivity analysis, those with overlapping samples in different articles, and those that did not report sufficient data for the meta-analysis after contacting the authors. We included eligible studies without group differences. If a study reported several neuroimaging metrics or independent patient samples, they were treated as separate data sets. Two authors (S.L. and Z.C.) independently completed the literature search. The results were compared, and any discrepancies were discussed and resolved by consensus. We conducted this meta-analysis according to the Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses (PRISMA) checklist (<xref rid="s1-50-2-e126" ref-type="supplementary-material">Appendix 1, Table S1</xref>, available at <ext-link xlink:href="www.jpn.ca/lookup/doi/10.1503/jpn.240112/tab-related-content" ext-link-type="uri">www.jpn.ca/lookup/doi/10.1503/jpn.240112/tab-related-content</ext-link>). The protocol was registered in PROSPERO (CRD42024607033). The specific measurements for resting-state BOLD signals in functional studies are shown in <xref rid="s1-50-2-e126" ref-type="supplementary-material">Appendix 1, Table S2</xref>.</p></sec><sec><title>Quality assessment</title><p>We estimated the quality of each selected study using a 10-point checklist by 2 authors, which mainly focused on the sociodemographic and clinical features, analysis methodology, image acquisition, and quality of reported results (<xref rid="s1-50-2-e126" ref-type="supplementary-material">Appendix 1, Tables S3, S4, and S5</xref>). Each item was scored as fully met (1), partially met (0.5), or unfulfilled (0). The scoring aimed to objectively estimate the rigour and completeness of included studies rather than critique the investigators or the work itself.</p></sec><sec sec-type="methods"><title>Statistical analysis</title><sec><title>Meta-analyses of structural and functional alterations</title><p>We employed the anisotropic effect size&#x02013;based signed differential mapping package to analyze structural and functional changes (<ext-link xlink:href="http://www.sdmproject.com" ext-link-type="uri">http://www.sdmproject.com</ext-link>). If studies do not report effect size measure of peaks, a &#x0201c;p&#x0201d; replaces the specific effect value for positive peaks, and &#x0201c;n&#x0201d; replaces the negative effect value. The software will conduct a pre-analysis to estimate effect sizes for these peaks. The procedures have been described in detail elsewhere.<xref rid="b54-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">54</xref>,<xref rid="b55-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">55</xref> In brief, the method uses peak coordinates and effect sizes to create the whole-brain effect size map and variance maps for each study. Subsequently, we generated the mean map by combining each study map through a random-effects meta-analytic model. Finally, we employed default thresholds (voxel threshold <italic>p</italic> &#x0003c; 0.005 with peak <italic>z</italic> &#x0003e; 1 and a cluster extent of 10 voxels) to show results in MNI coordinates.</p></sec><sec sec-type="methods"><title>Multimodal analysis of grey matter structure and function</title><p>We assessed brain regions with both functional and structural abnormalities between MDD-S and MDD-NS groups via the multimodal meta-analysis in the SDM package. By integrating the 2 probability maps of grey matter volume (<italic>p</italic><sub>GMV</sub>) and functional (<italic>p</italic><sub>F</sub>) alterations, the multimodal meta-analysis identifies a union of changes (U). The estimation of U is straightforward (U = <italic>p</italic><sub>GMV</sub> + <italic>p</italic><sub>F</sub> &#x02212; <italic>p</italic><sub>GMV</sub> &#x000d7; <italic>p</italic><sub>F</sub>) and is adjusted according to <italic>p</italic> = U + (1 &#x02013; U) &#x000d7; ln(1 &#x02013; U). The detailed processing procedures have been explained elsewhere.<xref rid="b22-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">22</xref> As recommended by a previous study, a more stringent statistical threshold (<italic>p</italic> &#x0003c; 0.0025) is adopted in multimodal analysis, which ensures that the false-positive rate is not increased compared with that in unimodal meta-analyses.<xref rid="b56-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">56</xref></p></sec><sec><title>Reliability, heterogeneity, and publication bias analyses</title><p>To assess the replicability and robustness of results, we performed whole-brain jackknife sensitivity analysis by repeating the same analysis one by one, discarding different data sets at a time to estimate the significance of results. Referring to previous work,<xref rid="b57-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">57</xref>,<xref rid="b58-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">58</xref> we used a threshold of 80% iterations.</p><p>We estimated between-study heterogeneity using the Cochran <italic>Q</italic> statistic. This heterogeneity <italic>Q</italic> statistic is assessed in terms of a &#x003c7;<sup>2</sup> distribution with degrees of freedom (the number value of the database reduced by 1), and a <italic>p</italic><sub>Cochran </sub><italic><sub>Q</sub></italic> less than 0.05 indicates significant between-study heterogeneity. The <italic>I</italic><sub>2</sub> statistic is used to assess the percentage of variation from heterogeneity, with values ranging from 0% to 100%; values around 25% indicate small amounts of heterogeneity, values around 50% indicate moderate heterogeneity, and values around 75% indicate greater heterogeneity.<xref rid="b59-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">59</xref></p><p>We detected publication bias in the meta-analysis using the funnel plot and the Egger test in the SDM software program, which extracts the values from statistically significant, relevant peaks. The degree of funnel plot asymmetry is measured by the intercept from regression of standard normal deviates against precision. In the absence of bias, the plot resembles a symmetric inverted funnel. The Egger test numerically analyzes bias results from funnel plots. Briefly, analysis results of <italic>p</italic> less than 0.05 and asymmetric funnel plots indicate publication bias in results from a specific brain region.<xref rid="b60-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">60</xref></p></sec><sec sec-type="methods"><title>Meta-regression analysis</title><p>To evaluate underlying effects of relevant sociodemographic and clinical variables, we conducted simple linear regression in SDM software. We used a more conservative threshold (<italic>p</italic> &#x0003c; 0.0005) to minimize spurious relationships.<xref rid="b61-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">61</xref> We used the relevant variables reported in at least 10 data sets to perform meta-regression analysis.<xref rid="b44-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">44</xref> In addition, only brain regions from the result of primary meta-analysis, which were detected in both of the extremes of the regressor and slope, could be recognized as the results of regression analysis.</p></sec></sec></sec><sec sec-type="results"><title>Results</title><sec sec-type="intro"><title>Sample characteristics in included studies</title><p><xref rid="f1-50-2-e126" ref-type="fig">Figure 1</xref> provides the PRISMA flowchart of the literature search, identification, and selection strategy. The 13 eligible studies (13 data sets) for GMV structure included 471 patients with MDD-S and 508 patients with MDD-NS. Sixteen studies (20 data sets) for GM function included 704 patients with MDD-S and 554 patients with MDD-NS. <xref rid="t1-50-2-e126" ref-type="table">Table 1</xref> summarizes the demographic and clinical characteristics and the results of quality assessments of included studies.</p></sec><sec><title>Regional differences in grey matter volume</title><p>Compared with patients with MDD-NS, patients with MDD-S showed increased GMV in the left calcarine fissure surrounding cortex (CAL), extending to the left lingual gyrus and left MOG (SDM <italic>z</italic> = 1.722, <italic>p</italic> = 0.00003, cluster size = 514), and the left postcentral gyrus (postCG), extending to the left preCG and inferior parietal gyri (IPG) (SDM <italic>z</italic> = 1.032, <italic>p</italic> = 0.001, cluster size = 510). Patients with MDD-S showed decreased GMV in the left CAU, extending to the left striatum (SDM <italic>z</italic> = &#x02212;2.139, <italic>p</italic> = 0.00009, cluster size = 449); the right IFG, extending to the right insula (SDM <italic>z</italic> = &#x02212;2.093, <italic>p</italic> = 0.0001, cluster size = 475); the right lingual gyrus, extending to the right cerebellum (SDM <italic>z</italic> = &#x02212;1.908, <italic>p</italic> = 0.0003, cluster size = 403); and the left fusiform gyrus, extending to the left cerebellum (SDM <italic>z</italic> = &#x02212;1.514, <italic>p</italic> = 0.002, cluster size = 430) (<xref rid="t2-50-2-e126" ref-type="table">Table 2</xref> and <xref rid="f2-50-2-e126" ref-type="fig">Figure 2</xref>).</p></sec><sec><title>Regional differences in functional activity</title><p>Relative to the MDD-NS group, the MDD-S group showed increased activity in the right insula, extending to the right rolandic operculum, right putamen, right heschl gyrus, right striatum, and right STG (SDM <italic>z</italic> = 1.781, <italic>p</italic> = 0.00005, cluster size = 1284); the right hippocampus, extending to the right cingulum (SDM <italic>z</italic> = 1.615, <italic>p</italic> = 0.0002, cluster size = 303); the left cerebellum, extending to the left inferior temporal gyrus (ITG) (SDM <italic>z</italic> = 1.402, <italic>p</italic> = 0.0007, cluster size = 219); the bilateral ITG, extending to the bilateral fusiform gyrus (SDM <italic>z</italic> = 1.249, <italic>p</italic> = 0.002, cluster size = 120); the left hippocampus, extending to the left cingulum (SDM <italic>z</italic> = 1.264, <italic>p</italic> = 0.002, cluster size = 73); and the right inferior temporal gyrus, extending to the right fusiform gyrus (SDM <italic>z</italic> = 1.177, <italic>p</italic> = 0.002, cluster size = 31). The MDD-S group showed decreased activity in right cuneus, extending to the right superior occipital gyrus, right precuneus, right superior parietal gyrus, and right CAL (SDM <italic>z</italic> = &#x02212;1.701, <italic>p</italic> = 0.0001, cluster size = 1022); the left IPG, extending to the left postCG and left supramarginal gyrus (SMG) (SDM <italic>z</italic> = &#x02212;1.843, <italic>p</italic> = 0.00004, cluster size = 647); the right SMG (SDM <italic>z</italic> = &#x02212;1.432, <italic>p</italic> = 0.002, cluster size = 126); the left middle frontal gyrus (MFG), extending to the left superior frontal gyrus (SDM <italic>z</italic> = &#x02212;1.386, <italic>p</italic> = 0.002, cluster size = 108); and the right MOG, extending to the right middle temporal gyrus (SDM <italic>z</italic> = &#x02212;1.460, <italic>p</italic> = 0.001, cluster size = 56) (<xref rid="t3-50-2-e126" ref-type="table">Table 3</xref> and <xref rid="f3-50-2-e126" ref-type="fig">Figure 3</xref>).</p></sec><sec sec-type="methods"><title>Multimodal analysis of grey matter structure and function</title><p>In comparison to the MDD-NS group, the MDD-S group showed increased GMV with hypoactivity in the left postCG, extending to the left IPG and left SMG; decreased GMV with hypoactivity in the right IPG, extending to the right SMG and right postCG; and decreased GMV with hyperactivity in the right insula, extending to the right putamen, right rolandic operculum, and right striatum (<xref rid="t4-50-2-e126" ref-type="table">Table 4</xref> and <xref rid="f4-50-2-e126" ref-type="fig">Figure 4</xref>).</p></sec><sec><title>Reliability, heterogeneity, and publication bias analyses</title><p>The whole-brain jackknife sensitivity analysis indicated that the GMV alterations in the left fusiform gyrus were significant in all but 2 combinations, and the other GMV changes were robust, found in all but 1 combination (<xref rid="s1-50-2-e126" ref-type="supplementary-material">Appendix 1, Table S6</xref>). The brain functional alterations in the left IPG, right insula, and left ITG were significant in all combinations of the data sets. The functional finding of right SMG was significant in all but 2 combinations, and all the rest of the functional alterations were preserved in all but 1 combination (<xref rid="s1-50-2-e126" ref-type="supplementary-material">Appendix 1, Table S7</xref>). All significant regions survived in more than 80% of iterations and thus exhibited high replicability and reliability.</p><p>The analysis of heterogeneity revealed that increased GMV in the left postCG, left preCG, and left IPG had moderate amounts of heterogeneity (<italic>I</italic><sup>2</sup> = 54.342%, <italic>Q</italic> = 26.580; <xref rid="t2-50-2-e126" ref-type="table">Table 2</xref>). All the other reported clusters showed low heterogeneity (<italic>I</italic><sup>2</sup> &#x0003c; 25%; <xref rid="t2-50-2-e126" ref-type="table">Table 2</xref> and <xref rid="t3-50-2-e126" ref-type="table">Table 3</xref>).</p><p>Egger tests indicated that the GMV changes in the left CAL and surrounding cortex (<italic>p</italic> = 0.02), the left CAU (<italic>p</italic> = 0.04), and the right IFG (<italic>p</italic> = 0.03) had statistical publication bias (<xref rid="t2-50-2-e126" ref-type="table">Table 2</xref>). As for the rest of clusters, Egger tests were not significant (<xref rid="t2-50-2-e126" ref-type="table">Table 2</xref>, <xref rid="t3-50-2-e126" ref-type="table">Table 3</xref>, and <xref rid="s1-50-2-e126" ref-type="supplementary-material">Appendix 1, Figure S1 and S2</xref>).</p></sec><sec sec-type="results"><title>Meta-regression results</title><p>Among patients with MDD-S, we separately evaluated the associations between alterations of GMV and function with mean age (available in 13 structural data sets and 16 functional data sets), percentage of female patients (available in 12 structural data sets and 16 functional data sets), and Hamilton Depression Rating Scale (HAMD) 17 scores (available in 11 functional data sets). Because of the complexity and insufficient data of the medication status and illness duration, we did not carry out meta-regression analyses for these variables. The results showed that spontaneous brain activity alterations in the right cuneus were negatively associated with mean age of the MDD-S group (<italic>x</italic> = 16, <italic>y</italic> = &#x02212;70, <italic>z</italic> = 38; 372 voxels; SDM <italic>z</italic> = &#x02212;3.035, <italic>p</italic> &#x0003c; 0.00002) (<xref rid="f5-50-2-e126" ref-type="fig">Figure 5</xref>). In addition, the results showed that the proportion of females was negatively related to brain activity in the left MFG (<italic>x</italic> = &#x02212;30, <italic>y</italic> = 58, <italic>z</italic> = 10; 24 voxels; SDM <italic>z</italic> = &#x02212;2.111, <italic>p</italic> &#x0003c; 0.0003) in MDD-S (<xref rid="f5-50-2-e126" ref-type="fig">Figure 5</xref>).</p></sec></sec><sec sec-type="discussion"><title>Discussion</title><p>In this voxel-based multimodal meta-analysis, we investigated joint and unique structural and functional alterations among patients with MDD with vulnerability to suicide. The main results revealed increased GMV and hypoactivity in the left postCG, decreased GMV and hypoactivity in the right IPG, and decreased GMV and hyperactivity in the right insula among patients with MDD-S compared with those with MDD-NS. In addition, compared with patients with MDD-NS, those with MDD-S showed increased GMV in the left CAL and decreased GMV in left CAU, right IFG, right lingual gyrus, and left fusiform gyrus; meanwhile, they also showed hyperactivity in the bilateral hippocampus, left cerebellum, and bilateral ITG, as well as hypoactivity in the right cuneus, right SMG, left MFG, and right MOG. Jackknife sensitivity suggested that the main findings were reproducible and robust. Furthermore, mean age and proportion of females showed a significant modulation effect on functional alterations in MDD-S, as revealed by meta-regression analyses.</p><p>Patients with MDD-S showed joint increased GMV with hypoactivity in the left postCG, left IPG, and left SMG compared with those with MDD-NS. We also found decreased GMV in the left preCG in the MDD-S group. The postCG plays an important role in somatosensory perception and emotional response.<xref rid="b77-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">77</xref>,<xref rid="b78-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">78</xref> The postCG has been thought to support processing sensory input in people who died by suicide. <xref rid="b79-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">79</xref> An fMRI study indicated that lower activity in the preCG and postCG may be associated with damaged motor and somatosensory function among patients with depression with high suicidal ideation.<xref rid="b80-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">80</xref> Abnormality in the preCG has been associated with deficits in impulsivity control and negative cognitive styles.<xref rid="b81-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">81</xref>&#x02013;<xref rid="b83-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">83</xref> Taken together, our results and those of previous studies support that anomalous structure and function in the left postCG and left preCG may be related to negative perception of sensory stimuli and deficits in inhibitory control in MDD-S. The underlying mechanism of increased GMV with hypoactivity may be that hypoactivity occurs earlier, followed by increased GMV in the same area as compensation or increased GMV may cause relative hypo-increase of blood flow and the working state of neurons in the same region deteriorates.<xref rid="b22-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">22</xref></p><p>We observed joint decreased GMV with hypoactivity in the right IPG, right SMG, and right postCG in the MDD-S group compared with the MDD-NS group. The IPG is mainly responsible for emotional body perception and processing, especially visual and haptic identification of the facial expression of emotions.<xref rid="b84-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">84</xref> One transcranial magnetic stimulation study found that stimulation in the IPG enhanced fearful body processing.<xref rid="b85-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">85</xref> An fMRI study reported substantial activation in the IPG in tasks related to facial expressions of emotion (disgust, neutral and happiness),<xref rid="b71-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">71</xref> which suggests that the IPL is primarily involved in processing of emotions. Therefore, alterations in the IPL may result in dysfunction of emotion perception and processing, and can easily lead people into related negative emotions or loss of pleasure and even suicidal behaviour. Moreover, an fMRI study found that abnormal activity of the SMG among patients with suicide attempt was associated with the experience of social rejection,<xref rid="b86-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">86</xref> an important factor associated with the risk of suicide, which indicated a role of SMG in sensitivity to social exclusion. The underlying mechanism of decreased GMV with hypoactivity may be that hypoactivity is caused by less neurogenesis or increased cell death, which ultimately leads to exhaustion and GMV atrophy.</p><p>We found GMV atrophy and hyperactivity in the right insula, right putamen, right rolandic operculum, and right striatum among patients with MDD-S compared with those with MDD-NS. As a key multimodal integration hub with connectivity to an extensive network of cortical and subcortical regions, the insula contributes to multiple functional dimensions ranging from sensation, emotion, and motivation to cognition.<xref rid="b87-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">87</xref> A meta-analysis of task-fMRI studies confirmed that increased activity in the insula is critical for dysfunction of self-awareness and emotion processing among patients with MDD with a history of suicide attempts.<xref rid="b88-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">88</xref> A structural MRI study revealed lower insula thickness among adults with MDD with suicidal ideation.<xref rid="b65-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">65</xref> Preliminary evidence has shown a relationship between altered insula volume and attempted suicide and impulsivity.<xref rid="b89-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">89</xref>,<xref rid="b90-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">90</xref> In terms of molecular mechanisms, patients with MDD with suicidal ideation have shown increased translocator protein activity in the insula relative to those without suicidal ideation, suggesting that the insula may induce vulnerability to suicide via a neuroinflammation pathway.<xref rid="b91-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">91</xref> The insula has been implicated in both suicide attempt and suicidal ideation and may be vital for the transition from ideation to attempt.<xref rid="b92-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">92</xref> In line with these findings, insula changes may underlie the neural mechanism of suicide in MDD and could be a prominent signature of MDD-S and a promising target for brain stimulation treatment of related disorders. Additionally, as a key site of the salience network, the insula cortex mediates between the extended ventromedial prefrontal cortex and the dorsal prefrontal cortex&#x02013;IFG system.<xref rid="b92-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">92</xref> Notably, our results showed abnormality in the right insula along with decreased GMV in the right IFG. The IFG participates in cognitive control and response inhibition.<xref rid="b93-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">93</xref>&#x02013;<xref rid="b95-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">95</xref> Neurobehavioural inhibition and impulsivity have been known to be associated with developing suicide risk.<xref rid="b96-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">96</xref> Therefore, the impairments in the dorsal prefrontal cortex&#x02013;IFG system and salience network may facilitate the progression from suicidal ideation to attempt via their role in cognition, emotion, complex decision-making, and planning.<xref rid="b92-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">92</xref></p><p>The dorsal striatum, including the CAU and putamen, is modulated by the substantia nigra<xref rid="b97-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">97</xref> and is involved in rewarding pathways, voluntary motor control, and cognition processes.<xref rid="b98-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">98</xref>,<xref rid="b99-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">99</xref> The striatum seems to be more important in suicidal behaviour under a depressive state than under general states.<xref rid="b53-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">53</xref> Lesions of the striatum mainly contribute to suicidal behaviour by increasing impulsivity.<xref rid="b99-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">99</xref> As a subregion of the striatum, the putamen is associated with motor skills and the process of rewards,<xref rid="b100-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">100</xref> and is a crucial component in frontal&#x02013;striatal circuitry. Structural alteration in the putamen is associated with an exaggerated preference for immediate rather than larger delayed rewards among patients with late-life depression with suicide attempt.<xref rid="b99-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">99</xref> In our study, the decreased activity and GMV of the CAU in the MDD-S group were not negligible. The CAU is a central component of dopaminergic reward systems and takes part in processing of positive rewarding stimuli.<xref rid="b101-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">101</xref> There is a negative correlation between altered CAU volume and function and anhedonic symptoms, as well as depressive severity.<xref rid="b102-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">102</xref>,<xref rid="b103-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">103</xref> We thus infer that abnormality in the CAU might contribute to suicide risk by impaired positive reward modulation and anhedonic symptoms. In short, abnormal function and structure in the striatum may contribute to instantaneous reward decisions and thus accelerate impulsive suicidal acts under a depressive state. In addition, decreased GMV with hyperactivity in some of the observed regions may indicate that the hyperactive neural activities induced GMV exhaustion or were compensatory to the GMV atrophy, which needs further investigation.</p><p>Based on our results, the 3 brain clusters with structural abnormalities accompanied by functional disturbances showed some diverse links between GMV increases or decreases and between hyperconnectivity or hypoconnectivity, rather than a unidirectional association, a finding that supports the close and complex correlation patterns of brain functional and structural changes in MDD-S. These regions are responsible for negative perception of sensory stimuli, impulsivity, and emotion processing disorder in MDD-S, which means that they play a substantial role in the pathological development of suicide in MDD and may serve as potential biomarkers for further exploration.</p><p>Inspired by the concept of topographic dynamic reorganization, proposed by Northoff,<xref rid="b104-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">104</xref> the co-occurrence of the different psychopathological symptoms in MDD may stem from their underlying global topographic and dynamic disbalances rather than from local deficits. Future research can use these colocalization regions in our meta-analysis to explore topographic and dynamic characterization, which will further help to investigate intrinsic or systematic relationships between the brain, symptoms, and behaviour in MDD-S.</p><p>Our results revealed increased GMV clusters in the left CAL in the MDD-S group, compared with the MDD-NS group. There are anatomic and functional correlations among the lingual gyrus, fusiform gyrus, MOG, and CAL and surrounding cortex. Anatomically, the medial surface of the occipital lobe has a characteristic calcarine fissure, which separates the medial surface of the occipital lobe into the lingual gyrus and cuneus; the fusiform gyrus lies below the lingual gyrus. Functionally, these brain regions belong to the visual cortex.<xref rid="b105-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">105</xref> The occipital lobe function involves visual stimuli processing, visual working memory&#x02013;related information consolidation, and face perception.<xref rid="b70-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">70</xref> The negative biases in facial emotion perception &#x02014; characterized by attentional biases for fearful but not sad or happy faces and recognition of happiness and neutral face as negative expressions &#x02014; might increase risk for suicide.<xref rid="b106-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">106</xref>&#x02013;<xref rid="b108-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">108</xref> Previous fMRI studies found that lower activation in the bilateral fusiform gyri extending to lingual gyri was linked with altered response to social&#x02013;emotional stimuli among patients with MDD with suicide attempts.<xref rid="b109-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">109</xref> Thus, the altered brain regions found in our analysis may bias perceptual sensitivity to negative expression and increase risk of suicide, which demands further exploration.</p><p>In our study, we observed significantly higher activation in the bilateral hippocampus, left cerebellum, and bilateral ITG, and lower activation in the right cuneus, right SMG, left MFG, and right MOG in the MDD-S group compared with the MDD-NS group. The hippocampus, an important part of the frontolimbic circuitry, is the main region for memory recall. <xref rid="b110-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">110</xref> It is involved in many other complex behaviours, such as regulating emotion responses, cognition navigating, sensorimotor integration, regulatory processes, and spatial goal coding.<xref rid="b111-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">111</xref>&#x02013;<xref rid="b113-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">113</xref> Abnormal activity in the hippocampus may be related to poor performance in memory, emotional disturbances, and cognitive deficits in MDD-S.<xref rid="b114-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">114</xref> The heschl gyrus &#x02014; a structure containing the primary auditory cortex and connecting regions within the sensorimotor, auditory and visual cortices<xref rid="b115-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">115</xref>,<xref rid="b116-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">116</xref> &#x02014; is located in the right STG and is an essential component of the auditory&#x02013;sensorimotor network, which involves acoustic processing.<xref rid="b116-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">116</xref> From a behavioural perspective, people who have difficulty in hearing and communication, might develop social isolation, loneliness, consequent cognitive decline, and depression.<xref rid="b117-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">117</xref> From a neurophysiological perspective, a kind of compensation relationship exists between the auditory pathway and cognitive control networks.<xref rid="b117-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">117</xref> The STG has been implicated in facial expression recognition, social cognition, and sound processing. It has been identified as a potential trait marker of suicide attempt, <xref rid="b118-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">118</xref>&#x02013;<xref rid="b121-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">121</xref> although few available studies have focused explicitly on the role of the heschl gyrus in the processing of auditory or cognition in depression with suicidality. The cingulate cortex &#x02014; with altered activity in the anterior and posterior cingulate cortex &#x02014; has been the subject of intense research on suicide-related networks. As a prominent hub in the frontal&#x02013;striatal circuitry, the anterior cingulate cortex acts as a bridge between the orbital frontal cortex and basal nuclei, combining cognitive activity with affective experience.<xref rid="b122-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">122</xref> The posterior cingulate cortex is a prominent hub within the default mode network. This network plays a prominent role in self-referential processes, autobiographical memory, social cognition, and prospective imagery, and has been involved in the pathophysiology of suicide across mental disorders.<xref rid="b123-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">123</xref>,<xref rid="b124-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">124</xref> In addition, the ITG participates in emotional processing.<xref rid="b125-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">125</xref> Alteration in the ITG may cause emotional distress, which is not only related to MDD itself, but has also been associated with suicide among patients with MDD.<xref rid="b126-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">126</xref> The ITG is considered 1 of the top 10 regions to predict suicide risk among patients with depression.<xref rid="b79-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">79</xref> The right superior parietal gyrus (especially the right precuneus), left MFG, and left superior frontal gyrus participate in the frontoparietal circuit. Abnormal function of this network has been reported to coincide with the impaired decision-making or problem-solving, lowered executive functioning, and cognitive inflexibility in those with suicide attempt.<xref rid="b36-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">36</xref> In our meta-regression analyses, we found that a greater proportion of females in the MDD-S group was associated with decreased functional activity of the left MFG, which indicates that hypoactivity in left MFG tended to occur more often in female patients with MDD-S. Older age was correlated with decreased function in the right cuneus, which suggests that functional activity changes in the right cuneus may become more pronounced with older age in the course of MDD-S.</p><sec><title>Limitations</title><p>Given a lack of data, we were unable to analyze the influence of clinical details such as disease duration, severity, suicidality items from the HAMD-17 scores, different suicidal gradients and medical treatments. Identifying heterogeneity in association with brain features and clinical parameters would lead to a better understanding of differences between patients with MDD-S and those with MDD-NS. Performing regression and subgroup analysis with more detailed information and larger samples is encouraged in future research, which will greatly contribute to a more comprehensive understanding of patients with MDD with different suicidal gradients. Although we found some brain regions with both structural and functional alterations, what remains unknown is causality between the structure and function of these regions. Despite the documentation of limited heterogeneity across all regions, with the exception of 1 cluster in the left postCG, left preCG, and left IPG (<italic>I</italic><sup>2</sup> = 53.342%, <italic>Q</italic> = 26.580), we found that an original study (which collected data from older male patients only) in our meta-analysis reported alteration in the left postcentral gyrus. Significant differences in sample characteristics may be the main reason for heterogeneity.<xref rid="b126-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">126</xref>,<xref rid="b127-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">127</xref> In addition, Egger tests showed that the left CAL and surrounding cortex, left CAU, and right IFG were subject to publication bias. This may be related to the fact that the small sample sizes limited statistical power to detect subtle brain differences, imputed as a null effect size, while larger samples may have yielded an artifact for larger effect sizes. Incomplete clinical information, suicide gradients, and sample heterogeneity are also possible reasons for publication bias.<xref rid="b127-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">127</xref>&#x02013;<xref rid="b129-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">129</xref> Although the variability across studies was considered and evaluated statistically, caution is advised. Further evaluation is needed when more published studies become available.</p></sec></sec><sec><title>Conclusion</title><p>We performed a voxel-based multimodal meta-analysis to provide a comprehensive overview of abnormalities in GMV and resting-state brain activity between MDD-S and MDD-NS. Our results suggested that the critical clue for conversion to suicidal ideation or suicide attempt among patients with MDD was characterized by joint alterations of regional structure and function abnormalities in the left postCG, right IPG, and right insula, as well as distinct alterations in GMV and resting-state brain activity in the bilateral parietal, occipital, and frontal lobe, and left thalamus, areas involved in frontolimbic circuitry, frontal&#x02013;striatal circuitry, the frontoparietal circuit, the salience network, the auditory&#x02013;sensorimotor network and the default mode network. These brain regions are associated with impulsivity, impaired positive reward modulation, emotional disturbances, abnormal emotion processing, and cognitive deficits in MDD-S. These findings support an understanding of the relationship between neural substrates and clinical symptoms among patients with MDD with suicidality and provide potential brain region targets for further clinical applications and scientific research. Specifically, these brain regions, especially those with abnormalities of both brain function and structure, may serve as intervention targets for transcranial magnetic stimulation therapy to alleviate or prevent suicidal ideation and attempt.<xref rid="b130-50-2-e126" ref-type="bibr">130</xref> These brain regions can be regarded as latent variables to establish mediating models and causal links for exploring the relationship between MDD-S, the brain, and behavioural factors. Future research should include prospective studies with large sample sizes to assess the predictive capacity of these neuroimaging findings and to elucidate the neuropathologic mechanism of multimodal and unimodal brain changes.</p></sec><sec sec-type="supplementary-material"><title>Supplementary Information</title><supplementary-material id="s1-50-2-e126" position="float" content-type="local-data"><media xlink:href="JPN-240112-at-1.pdf" id="d100e872" position="anchor"/></supplementary-material><supplementary-material id="s2-50-2-e126" position="float" content-type="local-data"><media xlink:href="JPN-240112-f1-longdesc.pdf" id="d100e874" position="anchor"/></supplementary-material></sec></body><back><fn-group><fn id="fn2-50-2-e126" fn-type="COI-statement"><p><bold>Competing interests:</bold> None declared.</p></fn><fn id="fn3-50-2-e126"><p><bold>Contributors:</bold> Ziqi Chen and Qiyong Gong conceived and designed the work. Shiqi Lin, Ziqi Chen, and Youjin Zhao contributed to data acquisition, analysis, and interpretation. Shiqi Lin drafted the manuscript. All of the authors revised it critically for important intellectual content, gave final approval of the version to be published, and agreed to be accountable for all aspects of the work.</p></fn><fn id="fn4-50-2-e126"><p><bold>Funding:</bold> This study was supported by the National Key R&#x00026;D Program of China (no. 2022YFC2009900) and the National Natural Science Foundation of China (nos. 81820108018, 81761128023 and 82027808).</p></fn><fn id="fn5-50-2-e126"><p><bold>Data sharing:</bold> This study collected and analyzed the summary statistics of previous published studies. All data collected are available in these included articles. All data analysed in this study are available from the corresponding author on reasonable request.</p></fn></fn-group><ref-list><title>References</title><ref id="b1-50-2-e126"><label>1</label><mixed-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Ebmeier</surname><given-names>KP</given-names></name><name><surname>Donaghey</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name><name><surname>Steele</surname><given-names>JD</given-names></name></person-group>. <article-title>Recent developments and current controversies in depression</article-title>. <source>Lancet</source>
